    "description": "Choroideremia-deafness-obesity syndrome (Ayazi syndrome) is a rare X-linked recessive contiguous gene deletion syndrome caused by an interstitial deletion at Xq21 that removes the choroideremia gene CHM (encoding Rab escort protein 1, REP1) together with neighbouring genes, including the DFN3 deafness gene POU3F4 and mental-retardation-region genes (e.g. RPS6KA6/RSK4, ZNF711). Affected males show the full choroideremia phenotype \u2014 progressive chorioretinal degeneration with childhood-onset night blindness, progressive peripheral visual field loss, and eventual central blindness \u2014 combined with congenital mixed (sensorineural and conductive) deafness with stapes fixation (DFN3), obesity, and moderate intellectual disability. The features beyond isolated choroideremia reflect the additional deleted genes in the contiguous segment, and the phenotype varies with deletion size and gene content. Female carriers show the typical patchy retinal changes of the choroideremia carrier state. The syndrome is distinct from isolated X-linked choroideremia (MONDO:0010557 / OMIM:303100); this contiguous-deletion form is OMIM:303110.\n",
