    "description": "Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI) is an antibiotic-associated colitis caused by the spore-forming, toxin-producing anaerobic bacterium Clostridioides difficile. Antibiotic exposure depletes the protective gut microbiota and the secondary bile acids it generates, causing loss of colonization resistance that permits ingested spores to germinate and the organism to expand. The vegetative bacteria secrete the glucosyltransferase exotoxins TcdA and TcdB (and, in hypervirulent strains, binary toxin CDT), which inactivate host Rho GTPases to cause epithelial cell death, barrier disruption, and intense neutrophilic inflammation. Clinically this produces watery or bloody diarrhea and pseudomembranous colitis, with a marked tendency to recur.",
