    "subtypes": [],
    "description": "Combined malonic and methylmalonic aciduria (CMAMMA) is an autosomal recessive metabolite-repair/proofreading disorder caused by biallelic ACSF3 variants. ACSF3 encodes a mitochondrial malonyl-CoA synthetase that also activates methylmalonate; loss of this activity impairs mitochondrial conversion of malonate and methylmalonate to their CoA thioesters. The biochemical signature is increased malonic acid and methylmalonic acid, usually with methylmalonic acid greater than malonic acid. Clinical expressivity is broad: selected clinical series include childhood metabolic and neurologic presentations and adult neurologic or psychiatric manifestations, while an unselected Quebec screening cohort had a favorable course in most patients.",
