    "updated_date": null,
    "subtypes": [],
    "description": "Childhood-onset neurodegeneration with progressive microcephaly (CONPM, MIM 619847) is an ultra-rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic variants in DTYMK, which encodes deoxythymidylate kinase (dTMPK, also called thymidylate kinase or TMPK). dTMPK catalyses the phosphorylation of dTMP to dTDP, the penultimate step in dTTP biosynthesis and the single point at which the de novo and salvage routes to dTTP converge, which makes the enzyme the bottleneck of the whole pathway. Affected children are born without malformations and with a head circumference in or near the normal range, then fail to make any developmental progress, develop severe and progressively worsening microcephaly, seizures, growth failure and a movement disorder that evolves from neonatal hypotonia to spasticity, and show dramatic postnatal atrophy of the cerebral hemispheres and basal ganglia while the brainstem is relatively spared. Death has occurred in the second or third year of life in the individuals with reported outcomes. The proximate mechanism is restriction of the dTTP pool available for nuclear DNA replication and repair, with consequent impaired DNA replication, elevated ribonucleotide misincorporation into genomic DNA, an abnormal DNA damage response, and apoptotic loss of neurons and oligodendrocytes.",
    "pathophysiology": [
      "Biallelic Damaging DTYMK Variants",
      "Loss of dTMPK Homodimerization",
      "Loss of dTMP Kinase Catalytic Activity",
      "Block of Canonical dTTP Biosynthetic Flux",
      "Impaired DNA Replication and Reduced Cell Proliferation",
      "Elevated Ribonucleotide Incorporation into Genomic DNA",
      "Genome Instability and Abnormal DNA Damage Response",
      "Apoptotic Loss of Neurons and Oligodendrocytes",
      "Progressive Postnatal Cerebral and Basal Ganglia Atrophy"
    ],
