    "description": "Focal cortical dysplasia type II, historically Taylor-type cortical dysplasia, is a localized malformation of cortical development and the most common cause of neocortical epilepsy in children. It is a mosaic mTORopathy: a somatic gain-of-function variant in an mTOR pathway activator, or a two-hit loss of function in one of the GATOR1 or TSC repressors, arises in a progenitor cell during corticogenesis and produces a clonal patch of cortex with constitutive mTORC1 signalling. That patch contains cytomegalic dysmorphic neurons and, in subtype IIb, balloon cells, and it is intrinsically epileptogenic. The resulting focal epilepsy is characteristically drug-resistant, and complete resection of the dysplastic tissue is the treatment that most reliably produces seizure freedom.",
    "pathophysiology": [
      "Somatic mTOR Pathway Variant in a Cortical Progenitor",
      "Constitutive mTORC1 Signalling in a Clonal Cortical Patch",
      "Dysmorphic Neuron and Balloon Cell Formation",
      "Disrupted Neuronal Migration and Cortical Lamination",
      "Intrinsically Epileptogenic Dysplastic Cortex",
      "Drug-Resistant Focal Epilepsy"
    ],
