    "description": "Konzo (cassavism) is an acute-onset, symmetric, permanent and irreversible upper motor neuron disease producing spastic paraparesis of the legs, epidemic and endemic in cassava-dependent regions of sub-Saharan Africa. It is caused by sustained monotonous consumption of insufficiently processed bitter cassava (Manihot esculenta) containing the cyanogenic glucoside linamarin, combined with a protein-poor diet low in sulfur-containing amino acids; the resulting chronic high dietary cyanide exposure cannot be adequately detoxified, leading to selective injury of upper motor neurons and the corticospinal (pyramidal) tracts with characteristic sparing of sensory and genitourinary function. Outbreaks cluster during droughts and food shortages and disproportionately affect children and women of childbearing age. Konzo is clinically near-identical to grass-pea neurolathyrism (a distinct toxic spastic paraparesis) and is distinguished from tropical ataxic neuropathy and HTLV-1-associated tropical spastic paraparesis. There is no cure; management is supportive and rehabilitative, and prevention relies on cassava detoxification (the wetting method) and dietary diversification.",
    "pathophysiology": [
      "Cyanogenic Glucoside (Linamarin) and Cyanide Exposure from Bitter Cassava",
      "Impaired Cyanide Detoxification with Low Sulfur Amino Acid Intake",
      "Upper Motor Neuron and Corticospinal Tract Injury",
      "Symmetric Permanent Spastic Paraparesis"
    ],
