    "description": "Middle East respiratory syndrome (MERS) is a severe viral respiratory infection caused by the Middle East respiratory syndrome coronavirus (MERS-CoV), a zoonotic betacoronavirus first isolated in 2012 from a patient in Saudi Arabia who died of pneumonia and renal failure. Dromedary camels are the reservoir for human spillover, and limited human-to-human transmission occurs, particularly in health-care settings. MERS-CoV enters host cells using dipeptidyl peptidase 4 (DPP4/CD26) as its receptor and causes a spectrum from asymptomatic infection to severe pneumonia, acute respiratory distress syndrome, and multiorgan failure, with a high case-fatality rate concentrated in older adults with comorbidities.\n",
