    "description": "Mixed neuronal-glial tumors (glioneuronal tumors) are a heterogeneous family of rare, predominantly low-grade (CNS WHO grade 1) primary central nervous system neoplasms composed of both neuronal and glial cell populations. They represent less than 2% of all primary CNS tumors, predominantly affect children and young adults, show a strong predilection for the temporal and frontal cortex, and are characterized by a frequent, often drug-resistant, focal epilepsy. Their shared pathogenic theme is somatic activation of the RAS-RAF-MEK-ERK (MAPK) signaling pathway, most commonly BRAF p.V600E in ganglioglioma and FGFR1 hotspot alterations in dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumor (DNET). The 2021 WHO CNS5 classification reorganized these entities into the \"glioneuronal and neuronal tumors\" family using integrated histological, immunohistochemical, and molecular (including DNA methylation) criteria.",
