    "updated_date": null,
    "subtypes": [],
    "description": "Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia, Al-Gazali type (also called Al-Gazali-Bakalinova syndrome, AGBK) is a rare autosomal recessive disorder characterized by multiple epiphyseal dysplasia, macrocephaly, and a distinctive facial appearance. It is caused by a biallelic missense mutation in KIF7, the human ortholog of Drosophila Costal2 and a cilium-tip regulator of the Sonic hedgehog (SHH) pathway. KIF7 dysfunction deregulates GLI transcription-factor output and impairs GLI3 processing, placing this disorder within the primary-cilium Hedgehog-signaling ciliopathy spectrum together with the allelic KIF7 disorders acrocallosal syndrome, fetal hydrolethalus syndrome, and a subset of Joubert syndrome. The skeletal (epiphyseal dysplasia) presentation is the distinctive feature that distinguishes this disorder from the other KIF7 ciliopathies. It is mechanistically distinct from the classic cartilage-matrix forms of multiple epiphyseal dysplasia (COMP, MATN3, COL9, SLC26A2), which act through chondrocyte matrix/ER-stress mechanisms rather than ciliary Hedgehog signaling.",
