    "description": "Oculopharyngodistal myopathy (OPDM) is a rare, adult-onset hereditary muscle disease caused by non-coding CGG-repeat expansions in the 5' untranslated region of distinct genes (LRP12, GIPC1, NOTCH2NLC, RILPL1, defining OPDM types 1-4). The expanded repeats are thought to be pathogenic through repeat-associated non-AUG (RAN) translation of toxic polyglycine-containing proteins and/or RNA toxicity, producing eosinophilic, p62/ubiquitin-positive intranuclear inclusions in muscle and other tissues. Clinically, OPDM presents with progressive ptosis, external ophthalmoplegia, facial and bulbar weakness (dysphagia, dysarthria), and distal limb weakness, often with elevated creatine kinase and a myopathic or rimmed-vacuole pattern on muscle biopsy.",
