    "description": "Spinal muscular atrophy with respiratory distress type 1 (SMARD1; also called autosomal recessive distal spinal muscular atrophy 1 / dSMA1, distal hereditary motor neuropathy type 6 / dHMN6, and severe infantile axonal neuropathy with respiratory failure type 1) is a rare autosomal recessive infantile motor neuron disease caused by biallelic loss-of-function variants in IGHMBP2 (immunoglobulin mu DNA binding protein 2), which encodes an ATP-dependent 5'->3' DNA/RNA helicase implicated in pre-mRNA processing and ribosomal translation. The disorder is characterized by degeneration of spinal anterior-horn lower (alpha) motor neurons with a characteristic distal and diaphragmatic predilection. Its hallmark is early infantile respiratory distress and failure from neurogenic diaphragmatic palsy (with radiographic diaphragmatic eventration), which\u2014together with weakness that begins distally in the limbs\u2014distinguishes SMARD1 from SMN1-related proximal SMA (in which the diaphragm is relatively spared and weakness begins proximally). Distal limb weakness and amyotrophy, areflexia, failure to thrive, and progressive autonomic and sensory involvement are common. SMN1 is normal. SMARD1 is allelic to the milder Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 2S and overlaps with the IGHMBP2-related autosomal recessive distal hereditary motor neuronopathy.\n",
